  "chapter": 742,
  "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
  "text": " \n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 107 Chapter 742\n \n       MEMORANDUM filed with Assembly Bill Number 6333, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  direct the department of environmental conservation to\n        study alternative municipal uses for recycled glass\"\n \n    APPROVED\n \n  This bill will require the Department  of  Environmental  Conservation\n(DEC)  in  consultation  with  the Department of Transportation to study\nalternative uses for recycled glass. DEC will also be  directed  by  the\nbill to issue a report based on the study's findings, submit such report\nto  the  Legislature,  as  well  as  publish  the report on the agency's\nwebsite.\n \n  Glass is the heaviest component of the municipal waste stream  and  is\ncostly  to  process.  In  2020,  DEC partnered with the State College of\nCeramics (NYSCC) at Alfred University to establish  the  NYS  Center  of\nGlass  Innovation  (CGI)  and invested $1.9 million through a three-year\nagreement with NYSCC to evaluate secondary markets for  glass.  I  agree\nwith  the  bill  sponsors'  interest  in finding viable uses of recycled\nglass. However, a legislative mandate for DEC to undertake  an  entirely\nnew  study  for  an  issue  already  being addressed would be costly and\nduplicative. Given the recent creation of the CGI and the robust invest-\nment by the State in identifying solutions, I am sure the  goals  sought\nby the bill sponsors can be achieved in a more cost-effective way.\n \n  To  that end, the Executive and the Legislature have agreed on changes\nto the current bill that will allow DEC to report  on  alternative  uses\nfor  recycled  glass based on the work currently conducted by NYSCC. The\nExecutive looks forward to future conversations with the Legislature  to\naddress the challenges of the recycling market.\n\n  On this basis, I am pleased to sign this bill.\n \n  This bill is approved.                         (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                                ______\n",
  "year": 2021
 },
